\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A negative determinant on a one-vertex circle}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use the real exchange fibre of \ref{fdip-011O} on the
one-vertex cellular circle in \ref{fdip-00U4} and fix scalar
#{+1}. There are two exterior slots, of degrees zero and one,
each carrying the two fibre lines. At identity link the
difference vanishes. Each slot then contributes
#{m^2-y^2}, giving determinant #{(m^2-y^2)^2}.}

\p{At the negative link the pullback minus the identity is zero
on the trivial fibre line and equals #{-2} on the sign line.
On the latter line #{\extD_U} is #{-2} times the exchange of
the two exterior slots. On an exterior exchange eigenline of
sign #{\epsilon=\pm1}, the full kernel on the two fibre lines is
##{\begin{pmatrix}m&y\\y&m-2\epsilon\end{pmatrix}.}
The product of its determinants for the two signs is
##{(m^2-y^2-2m)(m^2-y^2+2m)=(m^2-y^2)^2-4m^2.}
At #{m=1}, #{y=1/2} the determinants are #{9/16} at the
positive link and #{-55/16} at the negative link.
With unit bosonic weights they have opposite signs, so no common
normalization gives a probability law on these backgrounds.}
